0,Music of Tibet - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Music_of_Tibet,"May 6, 2026 - The music of Tibet reflects the cultural heritage of the trans-Himalayan region centered in Tibet, but also known wherever Indigenous Tibetan groups are found in Nepal, Bhutan, India and further abroad. The religious music of Tibet reflects the profound influence of Tibetan Buddhism on the culture.",Puroik,Sino-Tibetan
1,Puroik - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puroik,"July 30, 2025 - Puroik language, their Kho-Bwa (Sino-Tibetan) language · Category: Disambiguation pages · Search · Puroik ·",Puroik,Sino-Tibetan
2,Sino-Tibetan Branches Project (STBP) by Andy Hsiu - Puroik,https://sites.google.com/site/sinotibetanbranches/eastern-sino-tibetan/kho-bwa/puroik,"Puroik is clearly a Sino-Tibetan language, although it has undergone some sound changes that have created many unique-looking forms. Lieberherr's (2015) Proto-Puroik reconstructions are cited here. Some sound changes from Proto-Sino-Tibetan are (with Lieberherr's (2015) Proto-Puroik",Puroik,Sino-Tibetan
3,The Sound of Tibetan Pop - playlist by The Sounds of Spotify | Spotify,https://open.spotify.com/playlist/02lAxUTKaWXNEtYAIEaZpP,Playlist · The Sound of Tibetan Pop · 317 items · 243 saves,Puroik,Sino-Tibetan
4,Understanding the importance of Tibetan music,https://www.tibettravel.org/tibetan-culture/understanding-the-importance-of-tibetan-music.html,"May 6, 2025 - You will find that flutes and lutes combine with some percussion beats to create a soft aroma of music that is thought to resemble a constant communication link with the divine world, one that only ""perfect beings"" are able to compose. It is officially named Sino-Mongolian music and is heard in the streets during festivals and parties.",Puroik,Sino-Tibetan
5,Social:Puroik language - HandWiki,https://handwiki.org/wiki/Social:Puroik_language,"March 16, 2026 - The Puroik language (previously called Sulung, a derogatory term, by other tribes) is a set of language varieties spoken by the Puroik people of Arunachal Pradesh in India and of Lhünzê County, Tibet, in China. Besides their own language, the Puroik also use Nishi, Hindi, and Assamese.",Puroik,Sino-Tibetan
6,(PDF) Rethinking Sino-Tibetan phylogeny from the perspective of North East Indian languages,https://www.researchgate.net/publication/263852191_Rethinking_Sino-Tibetan_phylogeny_from_the_perspective_of_North_East_Indian_languages,"December 1, 2013 - with Aka fu (e.g. Miji ʃu, Koro sù, Puroik ʧa and Proto-Tani *ɕo), suggesting that this is",Puroik,Sino-Tibetan
7,Sino-Tibetan | Ethnologue Free,https://www.ethnologue.com/subgroup/236/,"Puroik suv, a language of India · Sartang onp, a language of India · Sherdukpen sdp, a language of India · Lepcha 1 · Lepcha lep, a language of India · Mijish 1 · Miju-Mishmi mxj, a language of India · Nungish 3 · Anong nun, a language of Myanmar · Drung duu, a language of China ·",Puroik,Sino-Tibetan
8,Songs and Music of Tibet | Smithsonian Folkways Recordings,https://folkways.si.edu/songs-and-music-of-tibet/world/album/smithsonian,"From a camp in the outskirts of Kathmandu, Howard Kaufman captures the essence of Tibetan chants. Kaufman gathers songs that are ""excerpts from much longer versions of both Eastern and Western Tibet"".",Puroik,Sino-Tibetan
